WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.560
Compare two career routes by looking at the steps, not just the destination, imagine Riley wants

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:07.480
to explore electrical work.

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:12.560
A career title does not tell Riley how to enter the field, what training is required, or

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:14.840
how much time and money each route needs.

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:17.120
Riley draws two possible routes.

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:21.520
One begins by investigating an apprenticeship and asking what a sponsoring employer would

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:22.520
require.

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:25.200
The other begins with a named training program.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:29.960
Both routes need current entry requirements, a credential or training outcome, and a plan

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:32.720
person or source who can confirm the next step.

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:35.240
Here is a fictional first-pass comparison.

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:38.400
Route A might need $900 for tools and travel.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:44.400
With the possibility of wages while learning, Route B might need $3,200 for fees and materials

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.880
and $1,800 for travel.

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:50.560
These are classroom figures, not actual Alberta fees.

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:52.920
Route A depends on a suitable placement.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:57.120
Route B depends on admission and on whether the program counts toward later training.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:59.200
Now make a decision that can change.

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:04.560
A student who needs income soon may investigate Route A first, but should not assume a placement

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:05.560
exists.

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:11.200
A student who can travel may investigate Route B, but should confirm recognition and total cost.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:14.360
Keep a source and access date beside every requirement.

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:17.720
Leave an honest question mark beside what is still unverified.

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:22.120
A good career plan is a testable next step, not a promise about the future.
